---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { PROFILE } from '../data/profile';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  description="How ORIZ.IN approaches authorship, AI assistance, sourcing, verification, corrections, advertising, and independence."
  lastUpdated="25 October 2026"
>
  <p>
    ORIZ.IN publishes practical articles, references, and tools under the
    editorial responsibility of {PROFILE.name}. This page explains the standards
    used before publication and the limits of what the site claims.
  </p>

  <h2>Editorial responsibility</h2>
  <p>
    The publisher is responsible for selecting topics, checking claims,
    publishing corrections, and deciding what is presented as complete. Authors
    are identified on articles. Where a technical review or external expert
    review occurred, it will be stated on the article; the absence of such a
    statement means no independent review should be assumed.
  </p>

  <h2>AI-assisted drafting</h2>
  <p>
    Some articles and tool descriptions may be drafted with the assistance of
    AI tools. AI output is treated as a starting point, not as a source. Drafts
    are checked against primary documentation, formal specifications, and the
    actual behavior of the tools in this repository. The publisher remains
    responsible for what is published.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site does not invent authors, quotations, citations, research findings,
    ratings, testimonials, traffic figures, or benchmarks. If a claim cannot be
    supported, it should be removed or clearly marked as opinion or
    interpretation.
  </p>

  <h2>Sourcing and verification</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Technical explanations link to primary sources such as specifications,
      official documentation, or standards whenever practical.
    </li>
    <li>
      API directory entries state the date on which the endpoint and relevant
      CORS behavior were checked, and distinguish documented behavior from
      observed behavior.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tool logic is covered by automated tests where practical, and tool pages
      disclose meaningful edge cases and limitations.
    </li>
    <li>
      Mathematical and financial explanations state their assumptions. A model
      is not presented as a prediction of future market performance.
    </li>
    <li>
      External data and quotations retain their licenses and attribution
      requirements. The site does not republish full third-party articles.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Originality and usefulness</h2>
  <p>
    Pages are written to answer a real question or support a real task, not to
    target a keyword with a thin summary. The site does not publish scraped
    articles, near-duplicate pages with superficial variations, fake tool
    interfaces, or placeholder legal pages to inflate its size.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
  <p>
    Substantive errors are corrected rather than quietly left in place. The
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a> explains how to report a
    problem. Where a page changes meaningfully, the updated date is revised.
    When a tool or API behavior changes, the accompanying article or reference
    may be updated as well.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence, ads, and sponsorship</h2>
  <p>
    ORIZ.IN is an independent project funded primarily by its publisher, with
    optional reader support. Advertising is disabled by default and will be
    labelled if enabled. Sponsorship or a commercial relationship must not buy
    a favourable recommendation, a fabricated test result, or removal of an
    accurate criticism. Sponsored content, if ever published, will be
    identified clearly.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy of tools and content</h2>
  <p>
    Tools are designed to process input locally unless a page clearly states
    otherwise. The editorial policy does not override the
    <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>, and an article should not encourage
    readers to paste sensitive production data into an interface that does not
    need it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Editorial questions, corrections, or disclosure requests:
    <a href={`mailto:${PROFILE.email}?subject=Editorial%20question`}>{PROFILE.email}</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
